On Sun, 2002-07-28 at 12:32, Westerman, Mark wrote:
> [...]
> Does anybody not see that when SCC was paid ( A lot of
> money) to build SELinux and delivered it to the NSA at 
> that point in time SELinux became GPL. They built a 
> product by modifing Linux (which is GPL) and sold it 
> to the NSA. 

Yes, this is correct.

> 
> SELinux is GPL and can be royalty-free distributed 
> under the GPL license. 

Yes, that is correct too, at the present time.  But if a software
patent arises (any software patent, not just the 3 mentioned) which
covers SE Linux and there is no royalty free license available, then
it will no longer be possible to distribute SE Linux without violating
the terms of the GPL.

> The GPL does make the SCC patent GPL, 

Did you mean "does not make" (as the "but" would seem to imply)?

> but all code based on SELinux is GPL. In other 
> words you cannot not build a new product using new 
> code and based on the SCC patent and sell it without 
> licensing the patent from SCC.
> 

This is correct, and reasonable (supposing that software patents are
reasonable).  This is what I meant when I said I thought the SCC lawyers
did their job.  They have granted a revocable royalty free license to
use the patents, without saying so in so many words (which might
interfere with it being really revocable, or more likely, might increase
the market share of SE Linux).  The GPL does not demand that patent
licenses be irrevocable, so they have done just enough to permit the NSA
to continue to distribute SE Linux.  I speculate that the contract
required them to do just this and no more.

Notice that on their web site, they make believe that there is no such
thing as SE Linux, and mention only their commercial products.  Another
advantage of this license which pretends not to be a license is that it
spreads Fear, Uncertainty, and Doubt about the future of SE Linux.  I am
certain that SCC would be ecstatic if all of the SE Linux developers
quit working on it and left it to wither so that SCC gets to keep the
money from NSA without losing any market share.

-- Ed Huff


